and $\boldsymbol{f}'$ at $\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -1\end{bmatrix}$ is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(-1) & 1^{2}\end{bmatrix}
$$

Suppose a transformation $\boldsymbol{f}:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ is defined by

$$
\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ x_{n}\end{bmatrix}
$$

where $f_1,f_2,\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\boldsymbol{f}'(\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \times n$ matrix

$$
\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\ \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\ \frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\end{bmatrix}
$$

In our case, we have the following:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} & \frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y} \\ \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x} & \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y} \\ \frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x} & \frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}\end{bmatrix}
$$

First, we compute the partial derivatives:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y, & \frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) = x, \\ \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2}) = 0, & \frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y, \\ \frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x}|= \frac{∂}{∂x}(y\ln x) = \frac{y}{x}, & \frac{∂f_{3}}{∂y}|= \frac{∂}{∂y}(y\ln x) = \ln x\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\ 0 & 2y \\ \frac{y}{x} & ln ⁡ x\end{bmatrix}
$$

Thus, the missing entries are $y,\:$ $0,\:$ and $\ln{x}$.

The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ is

$$
\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \nabla{f}(\mathbf{x}_n)
$$

where $H(\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\nabla{f}(\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\mathbf{x}_n$.

Equivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as

$$
H_f(\mathbf{x}_n)(\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \mathbf{x}_n) = -\nabla{f}(\mathbf{x}_n)
$$

In our case, the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}
∇f(x) &= \begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x) \\ \frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x)\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\ 2xy - x\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

and the Hessian is

$$
\begin{aligned}
H_{f}(x) &= \begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - x) \\ \frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - x)\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\ 2y - 1 & 2x\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\ 2y - 1 & 2x\end{bmatrix}
$$

Starting with $\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\ 2y - 1 & 2x\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\ 2xy - x\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\
\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1) - 1 \\ 2(1) - 1 & 2(1)\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} + 6(1) - (1) - 1 \\ 2(1)(1) - 1\end{bmatrix} \\
\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ -1\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Solving this system of equations, we get $\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\ -1/11\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_{1} &= x_{0} + \begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\ -1/11\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\ -1/11\end{bmatrix} \\
&\approx \begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\ 0.909\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

To compute $\nabla_{a,b} \: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{∂f}{∂a} &= \frac{∂}{∂a}(a - bx^{2} + y^{3}) = 1 \\
\frac{∂f}{∂b} &= \frac{∂}{∂b}(a - bx^{2} + y^{3}) =-x^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we obtain the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}
∇_{a, b}f &= \begin{bmatrix}\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\ \frac{∂f}{∂b}\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -x^{2}\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

To compute $\nabla_{\mathbf{w}} \: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $\mathbf{w}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{∂f}{∂w_{1}} &= \frac{∂}{∂w_{1}}(w_{1}x_{1}^{3} + w_{2}x_{2}^{3}) = x_{1}^{3} \\
\frac{∂f}{∂w_{2}} &= \frac{∂}{∂w_{2}}(w_{1}x_{1}^{3} + w_{2}x_{2}^{3}) = x_{2}^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, with $[3,-2]$ we obtain the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}
∇_{w}f &= \begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{3} \\ x_{2}^{3}\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}27 \\ -8\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$
